{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"Exit code 1\n# Third-party extension governance\n\n**Status:** design, 2026-08-23. Decisions 1–5 settled; the build is a handoff.\nCompanion analysis in turnbridge's `docs/LINEAGE_AND_COMPOUNDING.md` §4.\n\n## Problem\n\ncledger's extension point today is \"`kind` is an open string (`schema.ts:7-9`)\nand `content` is never interpreted.\" turnbridge uses it correctly, appending\n`continuation` events through the public `appendEvents`. But it is\n**ungoverned**, and that became load-bearing when a second tool took a\ndependency on it: context-graph's design parses turnbridge's `continuation`\npayload (`rel:\"continues\"`, `imported_through_seq`), and intent-recall is the\nlikely third. A private convention that other tools parse is a public\ninterface with no owner and no versioning.\n\n**Non-goal, explicitly:** cledger must not learn what a continuation *is*. It\nshould not gain `continues`, `continuation`, or any bridging concept in its own\nvocabulary — that inverts the dependency and asks cledger to document a\nrelation it has no use for and cannot validate the meaning of. Everything below\nstays agnostic about what a third-party kind means.\n\n## Decision 1 — producer identity stays OUT of `eventId`\n\n**Settled: no change. This is already correct.**\n\nRaised as \"should `producer.tool` join the identity subset so events are\nattributable to their writer?\" It should not, and the reasoning is already\nwritten down for the analogous case (`schema.ts:243-251`): folding late-added\nproducer fields into identity gives the very same source line a different id\nbefore and after the upgrade, so a rescan duplicates rather than dedups.\n\nThe deeper reason is stronger than the migration one. **Identity answers \"which\npiece of source material is this.\"** Two tools reading the same transcript line\n*should* produce the same id — that is what makes capture idempotent. Putting\nthe writer into identity would break that deliberately.\n\nThe collision this was meant to solve is not an id collision anyway. If two\nproducers write different `content` under the same `kind`, their ids already\ndiffer, because `content` is in the identity subset. The real collision is\n**semantic**: a consumer sees `kind: \"continuation\"` and cannot tell whose it\nis. That is an interpretation problem, and Decision 2 handles it.\n\n## Decision 2 — identify third-party kinds by `(producer.tool, kind)`\n\n**Settled: no namespacing, no rename, no migration.**\n\nAn earlier draft proposed namespacing the kind string itself\n(`turnbridge/continuation`). That is the wrong fix, and Decision 1 is what\nmakes it wrong: once producer identity is *not* an identity-subset concern,\nthere is no reason to smuggle the producer into a field that is. cledger\n**already carries the producer as structured data** on every event:\n\n```ts\nproducer: { tool, version?, source?, source_version?, model?, provider?, session_id? }\n```\n\n`producer.tool` is required by `validateEvent`, and turnbridge already sets it\n(`{ tool: \"turnbridge\", version, source: targetCli }`). The information a\nconsumer needs is present, typed, and populated today. Encoding it a second\ntime inside `kind` would be string-packing a field that already exists, and it\ncould not carry `producer.version` without getting worse (`turnbridge@0.3/…`).\n\nSo the contract is:\n\n> **A third-party event kind is identified by the pair `(producer.tool, kind)`,\n> never by `kind` alone.** `producer.version` states which revision of that\n> vocabulary the event was written against.\n\nWhat this actually costs, versus the rename:\n\n| | namespaced `kind` | `(producer.tool, kind)` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Event ids change | yes (`kind` is in identity) | **no** |\n| Repos needing migration | 9 (measured, below) | **0** |\n| Carries producer version | no | **yes, already** |\n| New fields | none | **none** |\n\nMeasured 2026-08-23 — repos in `~/repos/evintunador` carrying cledger notes:\n`conversation-ledger` (49 anchors), `ds4-gateway` (26), `turnbridge` (26),\n`ds4_custom` (10), `intent-recall` (10), `context-graph` (6),\n`AI-s_non-anthropomorphic_path_to_understanding` (2), `tagseq2tagseq` (2),\n`arxiv-summaries-workflow` (1). The rename would have had to run across all\nnine; the pair-key contract requires touching none.\n\n### The one real gap this leaves\n\n`ReadOptions` (`store.ts:258`) filters on `reachableFrom`, `kind`, `source`,\n`model`, and `conversation` — but **not `tool`**. So a consumer wanting\n\"turnbridge's continuation events\" must fetch by kind and filter client-side,\nwhich is exactly the papercut that made namespacing look attractive.\n\n**First, a fact that reframes what filters are worth adding:** `readEvents`\nalready fetches every event in scope and filters **in memory**\n(`store.ts:318`). A filter option therefore saves *zero* I/O — it is pure\nergonomics, and any consumer can already express any of these as\n`events.filter(...)` itself. That argues for adding filters with a cited\nconsumer need and resisting speculative ones, since public API surface is\ncheap to add and permanent to ke\n\n... [1174 characters truncated] ...\n\nt would not actually remove the\n  client-side pass it was meant to serve.\n- `since`/`until` — no consumer has asked, and it would need to resolve the\n  `occurred_at` vs `recorded_at` ambiguity to be meaningful.\n- `producerVersion` — a consumer handling two vocabulary revisions reads both\n  and branches on the version; filtering it out is the rarer need.\n\nAlso worth stating in the docs, since it is the tradeoff being accepted: two\nevents differing *only* by `producer.tool` dedup to one, because `producer.tool`\nis not in identity. That is the correct behavior — byte-identical content is the\nsame fact — but it should be documented rather than discovered.\n\n## Decision 3 — migration mechanism (not needed now, but named)\n\nDecision 2 removes the migration. The pattern is recorded here anyway, because\nthe next vocabulary change may not be so lucky, and because it already exists\nand should not be reinvented.\n\n`renormalize.ts` is the precedent: a kind rename is structurally identical to a\nformat-drift re-normalization — same source material, new correct form, old\nevent kept. Its invariants transfer unchanged:\n\n- **Append-only.** The old event is never deleted; it is superseded by a\n  `supersession` event carrying `links:[{rel:\"supersedes\", target}]`.\n- **Idempotent.** Deterministic ids mean a second run is a no-op.\n- **Id fidelity.** The rewritten event gets the id a fresh write would produce.\n- **Redaction safety.** It rides the normal `appendEvents` redaction path.\n\nRouting caveat if it is ever needed: `renormalize` dispatches through\n`RENORMALIZERS`, keyed by `producer.source` and hardcoded to cledger's own\ncapture adapters, so a third-party migration does not route through it as\nwritten. The producer should own its own migration, consistent with a producer\nowning its vocabulary and its reader.\n\n## Decision 4 — registration, in the consumer-discovery form\n\n**Settled: persisted, discoverable — not process-local.**\n\nA producer registers `{ tool, kind, version, schema }`, persisted in repo\nconfig so that a **consumer can enumerate which third-party kinds a ledger\ncontains and who owns them**, without knowing what to look for in advance.\n`finalizeEvent`/`validateEvent` enforce a registered kind's declared shape\n**without cledger interpreting the content**.\n\nThe process-local alternative (register at runtime, validate on write) was\nconsidered and rejected: it buys write-side validation only, and the actual\nrequirement is context-graph being able to discover turnbridge's vocabulary\nrather than hardcode it. Discovery is the whole point.\n\nConstraints:\n\n- **Unregistered kinds stay fully storable.** Registration buys validation and\n  discoverability; it is never a precondition for writing. This preserves the\n  existing \"store and surface what you do not understand\" contract that\n  `unrecognized` and `raw` embody.\n- **Reads stay tolerant.** An unknown or unregistered kind is never a read\n  error.\n\n## Decision 5 — disclose the `ensureTransport` side effect\n\n**Settled: document it loudly; do not add an opt-out.**\n\nRestating this plainly, since the earlier phrasing was compressed:\n\n`appendEvents` calls `ensureTransport(repo, config)` at `store.ts:172` — on the\n**first append in a repo**, this installs a git **pre-push hook** and adds a\n**fetch refspec** to the repo's git config. From then on, ordinary `git push`\nalso pushes ledger notes and ordinary `git fetch` also fetches them. That is\nhow cledger's \"optional-but-default-on\" transport is wired up.\n\nThe issue is not that it is wrong — it is deliberate and correct for cledger's\nown capture path. The issue is **who inherits it**. `appendEvents` is a public\nAPI, so *any* third-party tool that appends a single event thereby modifies the\nuser's git config and installs a hook. Calling one function named \"append\nevents\" should not be how a tool discovers it has enabled network sync.\n\nRecommendation: document it prominently as part of the API contract rather than\nadding an opt-out flag. A producer appending events genuinely does want them to\nsync, so suppressing it would mostly create a footgun in the other direction —\nevents written that never leave the machine. It simply must not be a surprise.\n\n## Handoff build\n\n1. `ReadOptions` additions, wired into the filter at `store.ts:318` (+ tests):\n   `tool?: string`, `kind?: string | string[]` (widening the existing option,\n   backwards-compatible), and `linkRel?: string`.\n2. Registration: persisted `{tool, kind, version, schema}` in repo config, a\n   discovery API for consumers, and shape validation in\n   `finalizeEvent`/`validateEvent` for registered kinds only.\n3. Read-path tolerance tests: unknown/unregistered kind is never an error.\n4. Docs — the `(producer.tool, kind)` contract; that `producer.tool` is not in\n   identity and what that implies for dedup; registration and discovery; and\n   the `ensureTransport` side effect.\n5. No data migration. No event ids change. Nothing to run across the nine repos.\n(eval):1: ==TURNBRIDGE not found","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_0148ohgtnqSVy8SQTEGcQc7G","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"324e5de5bd856853a4c994915cf2b93c8a3d6e6b","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:a044dbb5-5e38-4400-bb2a-97061ee33f87","seq":25},"id":"ev1-027e97e8d8ced574f3049685ca64c4650d5a963c130587ece992b94af98142f0","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-24T02:18:14.090Z","producer":{"session_id":"a044dbb5-5e38-4400-bb2a-97061ee33f87","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.241","tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"Exit code 1\n# Third-party extension governance\n\n**Status:** design, 2026-08-23. Decisions 1–5 settled; the build is a handoff.\nCompanion analysis in turnbridge's `docs/LINEAGE_AND_COMPOUNDING.md` §4.\n\n## Problem\n\ncledger's extension point today is \"`kind` is an open string (`schema.ts:7-9`)\nand `content` is never interpreted.\" turnbridge uses it correctly, appending\n`continuation` events through the public `appendEvents`. But it is\n**ungoverned**, and that became load-bearing when a second tool took a\ndependency on it: context-graph's design parses turnbridge's `continuation`\npayload (`rel:\"continues\"`, `imported_through_seq`), and intent-recall is the\nlikely third. A private convention that other tools parse is a public\ninterface with no owner 