e), that's zero cledger work and the turnbridge doc already endorsed it.\n\n**4. If a registry does happen, where should it live?** Committed `.cledger.json` (travels with clones, but versioning conflicts as above), or the tool registers at runtime on first append (process-local, what the doc rejected)? The doc's stated reason for persistence is discovery — but a consumer could just as well discover vocabularies by reading distinct `producer.tool` values from the events themselves, with no registry.\n\n**5. Does \"the handoff is settled\" match your understanding?** Both docs say decisions 1–5 are settled and the build is ready to hand off. You said you haven't read them. I'd treat nothing as settled until you say so — is that right, or did you sign off on any of it in an earlier session?\n\n**6. Scope check on the turnbridge side.** The turnbridge docs' sections B (\"stays with us\") contain real bugs — turnbridge ignores `producer.model`/`provider` that cledger has shipped since 0.12.0 and still guesses the provider by string-splitting the model id; and the \"copy from the original, never from the copy\" fix for compounding degradation. Neither needs cledger changes. Do you want those on the table, or is this session strictly cledger?\n\nMy recommendation if you want a default: do the `readEvents` filters and the docs (items 1, 3, 4, 5 of the handoff), skip the registry, and add a note to the turnbridge TODO that `readLineage` is the published reader for `continuation`. That's small, un-speculative, and closes the only papercut with a real user today.\n\nresult: Reviewed both design branches; the cledger handoff is mostly no-ops plus three cheap read filters, and its one real build (a schema registry) is motivated by consumers (context-graph, intent-recall) that have no code yet — six clarifying questions above, recommended default is filters + docs, no registry.","type":"text"}],"diagnostics":null,"id":"msg_011CeLn8k9UD84kDxASYREKB","model":"claude-fable-5","role